# Kitty is the terminal frontend; herdr owns panes, tabs, and persistence.

# A window is a plain login shell for the duration of the multiplexer trial.
#
# Zellij needed `shell zjshell` because a Zellij session is per-window: without a
# wrapper putting one there, a window had no multiplexer and no persistence.
# herdr inverts that — one server, already running as a systemd user unit, that
# `herdr` attaches to. There is nothing for a window to arrange, so it does not
# try, and windows that are not the herdr window stay ordinary terminals.
#
# `.` means the login shell from SHELL or /etc/passwd, run as a login shell,
# which is what makes ~/.bash_env's PATH available — the same reason
# Ctrl+Shift+Y's `bash -l` below spells it out.
#
# Reverting to Zellij is this one line: {{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.local/bin/zjshell
shell .

font_family JetBrainsMono Nerd Font Mono
font_size 10.5

# Kitty ships no colour scheme here, so pane *contents* were on kitty's stock
# palette — #dddddd on #000000. That grey-on-black is a separate surface from
# the herdr theme below/above it: herdr only paints its own chrome, everything
# inside a pane is kitty's. Both had to move to fix "grey on black".
foreground #ffffff

cursor_shape block
cursor_blink_interval 0
scrollback_lines 10000
wheel_scroll_multiplier 3.0

window_padding_width 2
remember_window_size yes
initial_window_width 140c
initial_window_height 42c
tab_bar_min_tabs 2

enable_audio_bell no
allow_remote_control no
shell_integration enabled

# herdr owns persistence — its server is a systemd user unit, so it outlives every
# window — and closing a window only detaches the client. Nothing
# is lost, and the "it is running: ..." prompt is pure friction.
confirm_os_window_close 0

# ctrl+shift+enter and ctrl+shift+t are kitty's own again — a new window and a
# new tab, both plain login shells like every other one here.
#
# They used to run `hnew`, which opened a window on its own named herdr session.
# The case is real: two herdr clients attached to the SAME session are fully
# synced, so a second window showing separate work cannot just run `herdr`. But
# the cheap unit of independent work under herdr is a workspace inside the one
# session (`prefix+shift+n`), which needs no window at all, and that covers it
# in practice — the bindings went unpressed. They also only ever existed on a
# desktop: a box reached over SSH has no kitty to press them in, which is most
# of this fleet.
#
# Deliberately NO F-key bindings here, and this is load-bearing rather than an
# omission. F2/F3/F4 (new tab, previous tab, next tab) belong to the
# multiplexer's bare function-key layer — Zellij's before, herdr's now — and
# Kitty swallows a mapped key before any application in the window sees it.
# Binding them here does not shadow the multiplexer's versions: it deletes them,
# silently, with the tab bar still drawn and the keys simply dead. (Found the
# hard way when a trialled multiplexer bound all three here, which made it and
# Zellij mutually exclusive rather than merely both-installed.)
#
# The same rule applies one layer down and is worth knowing while both
# multiplexers are installed: a herdr running INSIDE a Zellij session never sees
# F2/F3/F4 either, because Zellij's `shared_except "tmux"` block claims them
# first. `shell .` above is what keeps that from happening by default — a plain
# window is nobody's session, so the first multiplexer in it is the only one.

# The only F-key mappings here, and the exception that the rule above is about.
#
# herdr enables the kitty keyboard protocol on this terminal, and under that
# protocol kitty spells F1/F2/F4 as bare `CSI P` / `CSI Q` / `CSI S` — the
# legacy spelling is SS3 `ESC O P/Q/S`, and F3 is `CSI 13~` in both modes.
# herdr's client parses the SS3 and `CSI <n>~` families and drops the bare-letter
# form, so F1, F2 and F4 die while F3 and F5-F12 (all `CSI <n>~`) work.
#
# Measured, not inferred: `kitten @ send-key F2` into a herdr client did nothing;
# raw `ESC O Q` and `ESC [12~` each opened a tab; raw `ESC [ Q` did nothing —
# identically on herdr 0.7.5, 0.8.2 and 0.9.0 under kitty 0.48.1. This block
# used to blame a `CSI R` / cursor-position-report collision for F3 alone; kitty
# never sends `CSI R` for F3, so the F3 line below was a no-op that happened to
# spell out what kitty already sent, and F1/F2/F4 were dead the whole time.
#
# `send_text` is why this does not fall foul of the "binding an F-key here
# deletes it" rule above: an *action* mapping consumes the key, but send_text
# re-emits bytes, so herdr still receives the key — spelled in the SS3 form it
# does parse (all four verified: `ESC O P/Q/R/S` -> the bound action, on 0.9.0).
#
# SS3 rather than the xterm `CSI 11~..14~` form, which herdr also parses,
# because this mapping fires in EVERY kitty window, not just the herdr one. SS3
# is what kitty's own terminfo advertises (kf1=\EOP .. kf4=\EOS), so an ncurses
# program in a plain window — mc, htop, an SSH session's tools — still sees
# F1-F4. Measured: ncurses getch() on TERM=xterm-kitty returns KEY_F1 for
# `ESC O P` and a bare ESC followed by `[11~` for `CSI 11~`. It is also exactly
# what kitty sends in legacy mode, so a window with no kitty-protocol
# application in it is unchanged by these lines.
#
# Remove these the day bare `CSI P/Q/S` parse upstream; `ctrl+space c/p/n` is
# the fallback that needs no terminal cooperation.
map f1 send_text all \x1bOP
map f2 send_text all \x1bOQ
map f3 send_text all \x1bOR
map f4 send_text all \x1bOS

# The deliberately un-multiplexed window, and the only one on this machine.
#
# This mapping predates the herdr trial, during which `shell` is a plain login
# shell and so every window is already un-multiplexed. It is kept because the
# reasoning outlives the trial: multiplex at exactly one end, and for a remote
# host that end is the remote one, because that is where persistence across a
# dropped connection has to live. A detach exists for when nesting happens anyway,
# but the better answer is not to nest — multiplex at exactly one end, and for a
# remote host that end is the remote one, because that is where persistence
# across a dropped connection actually has to live.
#
# So: open this window, SSH from it, and the remote multiplexer is the only one in
# the stack. Every key, the mouse, the clipboard, and the scrollback belong to it
# unambiguously. The cost is that this window has no local splitting — use
# another window, which is what `confirm_os_window_close 0` makes cheap.
#
# `bash -l` spelled out rather than inherited: `launch` overrides the configured
# shell, and a login shell is what makes ~/.bash_env's PATH available here. While
# `shell` above is `.` this is the same thing a plain window gets, so the mapping
# is currently redundant — kept because the reasoning outlives the arrangement,
# and it is the one window guaranteed to be nobody's session.
map ctrl+shift+y launch --type=os-window --cwd=current bash -l

# The same window, but going straight to a host: an fzf picker over ~/.ssh/config
# and the `ssh` lines in history, which then `exec`s, so the remote is the
# window's own process and ending it closes the window.
#
# Ctrl+Shift+Y plus a remembered hostname is two steps, and the two-step version
# of the right answer loses to the one-step version of the wrong one — SSHing from
# an ordinary pane, which nests. This makes not nesting the cheaper habit.
#
# Displaces Kitty's start_resizing_window, which is meaningless here: the
# multiplexer owns panes and tabs, so Kitty's own layout keys have nothing to
# act on.
map ctrl+shift+r launch --type=os-window --cwd=current {{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.local/bin/sshz
